    {
      "claim": "Storm Boris brought wind gusts of over 150 km/h to Vienna, Austria, in mid-September 2024.",
      "verdict": "contradicts",
      "rationale": "GDACS confirms Storm Boris caused a major flood event (Orange alert) across Austria, Czech Republic, Germany, Poland, Romania and Slovakia from 2024-09-12 to 2024-09-18, corroborating the storm's occurrence and severity as a rain/flood event. However, the specific wind-gust claim of 'over 150 km/h' in Vienna is not supported by any wind data source. ERA5 reanalysis at Vienna's coordinates (48.21N, 16.37E) shows a peak gust of only 90.0 km/h on 2024-09-14, with sustained wind peaking at 51.9 km/h. Station observations corroborate this: Wien/Hohe Warte (4.7 km from Vienna center) measured a peak gust of exactly 90.0 km/h on 2024-09-14 (with heavy rainfall of 101.6 mm that day and 207.9 mm total over the week), and the more distant St. P\u00f6lten Landhaus station (55.1 km away) recorded a slightly higher peak gust of 91.1 km/h with 420.1 mm total precipitation. The maximum measured or modeled gust anywhere near Vienna (91.1 km/h) is roughly 59 km/h below the claimed 150 km/h threshold -- a difference far too large to attribute to normal measurement/siting variance or generous interpretation of claim phrasing. While Storm Boris was indeed a significant and damaging flood-producing storm, the specific wind gust magnitude claimed (over 150 km/h) in Vienna is contradicted by both reanalysis and station-measured data.",
